

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that settles the target: a general mechanism, not one fitted to two known forms. That makes YAGNI the main
  risk, so the next question is where to draw the line on the rule vocabulary.

  The fork: which rules ship in the shared module versus which callers write themselves. This one is somewhat sticky —
  the rule-expression format becomes an interface every form depends on, so adding rules later is easy, but changing how
  a rule is [3mwritten[0m means touching every form already using it. What's cheap to change later: the set of built-in
  rules. What's expensive: the shape callers write them in.

  - [1mMinimal + escape hatch[0m — built-ins: [38;5;153mrequired[39m. Everything else is a plain function you pass in. Almost nothing to
    design wrong, and the escape hatch means nothing is blocked. Cost: every form re-writes its own email check, which
    is the duplication you're trying to kill.
  - [1mCore set + escape hatch[0m — built-ins: [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m/[38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, [38;5;153mmatches[39m (confirm-password), plus
    custom functions. Covers the large majority of web forms without speculating. Cost: a handful of rules that may sit
    unused for a while.
  - [1mBroad set[0m — the core set plus numeric ranges and async rules (server-side "is this username taken"). Cost: async
    validation drags in pending states, race handling, and debouncing — a real chunk of complexity for a need you
    haven't hit.

  My recommendation: the middle one. The escape hatch is what actually prevents over-building — it means an
  unanticipated rule is never blocked, so the built-in set doesn't have to be exhaustive to be useful. I'd specifically
  leave async out; it's the one piece here that changes the module's whole shape, and it's easy to add as a separate
  concern once a real case exists.
